capability route/match  the operation a request names: its path among the templates under each server's path, the most literal first, then its method; 404 and 405 told apart
  heroes  openapi.read_request, openapi.request_errors_of
  go      routers.Router, routers.Route, routers.RouteError, routers.ErrPathNotFound, routers.ErrMethodNotAllowed, gorillamux.NewRouter, gorillamux.Router, legacy.NewRouter, legacy.Router, legacy.Routers
  rust    -
  python  openapi_core.templating.paths.exceptions.PathNotFound, PathsNotFound, OperationNotFound, ServerNotFound, PathError
  note    A variable's value takes the longest text that lets the rest of its segment match, as gorilla/mux does, and never splits a character

capability route/web  every operation added to a `web` router or group, named by its operationId, under its first server's path
  heroes  openapi.add_routes, openapi.pattern_of, openapi.route_name_of
  go      -
  rust    aide axum::ApiRouter, ApiMethodRouter, ApiMethodDocs, RouterExt, routing::get, get_with, post, post_with, put, put_with, patch, patch_with, delete, delete_with, head, head_with, options, options_with, trace, trace_with, TypedPath, typed_path
  python  fastapi.openapi.utils.generate_operation_id, generate_operation_id_for_path, get_fields_from_routes
  note    The reverse of aide's: the routes come from the document. A template with a variable beside text in one segment, `/files/{name}.json`, is refused, which a `web` pattern does not say. In a `web` group a route keeps the path the document says a client asks, which stands under the group's prefix segment for segment, and joins the group; a path the group's prefix does not lead is refused `invalid` rather than moved, since the validator reads a request by the document's paths, and every route or none is added

capability validate/parameters  a request's path, query, header and cookie parameters read by their style and explode rules, typed by their schemas, validated, a default given where one is missing
